<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Entitlements for com.twinvpn.app.sysext — the NE packet-tunnel SYSTEM
  extension.

  Authority: ADR-0016 §11.9's macOS row (verbatim below), §12.6 / MX-1,
  P-06; ADR-0020's macOS rows for the two storage shapes; ADR-0021
  (notarization).

  ADR-0016 §11.9's macOS row, verbatim, for this component:

    "sysext: hardened runtime, library validation **on**,
     `disable-library-validation` / `allow-dyld-environment-variables` /
     `allow-unsigned-executable-memory` / `get-task-allow` all **absent** in
     release; `com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension =
     [packet-tunnel-provider-systemextension]`"

  Every one of those four "absent" entitlements is absent from this file, and
  their absence is the point: each one, if present, would re-open the code-
  injection surface that ADR-0016 §11.9 closes and that ADR-0018 §11.16 (f)
  depends on ("MUST NOT require writable-executable memory"). A reviewer should
  check this file for what is NOT here at least as carefully as for what is.

  ADR-0016 P-06 is the standing risk: "Apple grants the NetworkExtension
  entitlement for `packet-tunnel-provider-systemextension` (macOS)". Until they
  do, this file describes a build that cannot be signed. Nothing here has been
  signed, submitted, or validated by this domain.
-->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
      THE ONE THAT APPLE MUST GRANT.

      `packet-tunnel-provider-systemextension` is the SYSTEM-extension value.
      The bare `packet-tunnel-provider` value is the iOS / app-extension one;
      ADR-0016 §11.2 uses both, one per platform, and they are not
      interchangeable.

      Without it: systemextensionsd refuses the activation request and the
      provider never runs. There is no degraded mode — the tunnel simply does
      not exist. ADR-0016 §14(2) makes the grant a falsifiable schedule trigger
      and names MX-3 (a LaunchDaemon owning utun directly) as the fallback,
      never MX-2.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension</key>
    <array>
        <string>packet-tunnel-provider-systemextension</string>
    </array>

    <!--
      The VPN API entitlement.

      ADR-0016 §11.9 lists `com.apple.developer.networking.vpn.api = [allow-vpn]`
      explicitly for iOS/iPadOS and does NOT list it in the macOS row.

      UNVERIFIED: whether a macOS packet-tunnel system extension needs it. It is
      required to install a NETunnelProviderManager configuration, which the
      CONTAINING APP does (see TwinVPNApp.entitlements), so the app carries it
      and this bundle may not need it at all. It is included here because a
      missing VPN entitlement fails at profile-install time with an opaque
      NEVPNError rather than at build time, and a granted-but-unused entitlement
      is a smaller defect than a missing required one. **If a Mac build shows it
      is unnecessary, delete it** — an unnecessary entitlement is still surface.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.developer.networking.vpn.api</key>
    <array>
        <string>allow-vpn</string>
    </array>

    <!--
      The keychain access group shared with the containing app.

      ADR-0016 §11.4 records the residual this creates, and it is NOT hypothetical
      on macOS: "the keychain access group cannot be scoped below app identity",
      so anything in this group can use DeviceKey as a signing oracle.

      On macOS the mitigation ADR-0020's macOS row supplies is that the
      DEVELOPER-ID DAEMON shape does not use this group at all — it uses the
      System keychain (/Library/Keychains/System.keychain) with an item ACL
      created by SecAccessCreateWithOwnerAndACL and bound to the Team-signed
      binary, which IS scopable below app identity. This group therefore holds
      only items that the app and the extension genuinely share, and NEVER the
      identity key.

      ADR-0016 §11.5's HC-1 row is the rule this discharges: the key handle MUST
      be openable by the authority with no user logged in, and MUST be
      UNOPENABLE by the unprivileged client. A per-user keychain cannot do the
      first; this group cannot do the second. Only the System keychain does both,
      which is why the identity lives there and not here.
    -->
    <key>keychain-access-groups</key>
    <array>
        <string>$(AppIdentifierPrefix)com.twinvpn.shared</string>
    </array>

    <!--
      The app group, shared with the containing app.

      Used for the diagnostic ring-buffer hand-off and for nothing else on
      macOS. ADR-0016 §11.2's iOS table puts bundle generation and redaction in
      the APP because of the extension's memory ceiling; macOS has no such
      ceiling, but keeping the split identical across the two Apple platforms is
      what stops the diagnostic path being two different programs.

      NOT used for the store root. ADR-0020's macOS row puts the store root at
      /Library/Application Support/TwinVPN/ (root:wheel 0700), which is outside
      any app group container — because an app-group container is user-writable
      by construction and a root authority must not read its state from a
      directory an unprivileged process can rewrite.

      UNVERIFIED: the `group.` prefix requirement and whether App Groups behave
      identically for a Developer-ID (non-App-Store) macOS build. Apple documents
      App Groups for macOS as requiring a Team-ID prefix rather than the `group.`
      prefix used on iOS; both spellings appear in Apple's own samples.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
    <array>
        <string>$(TeamIdentifierPrefix)com.twinvpn.shared</string>
    </array>

    <!--
      DELIBERATELY ABSENT, each named because its absence is a requirement and
      not an omission (ADR-0016 §11.9's macOS row):

        com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation
          - would let an unsigned or differently-signed dylib load into a root
            process. §11.9 requires library validation ON.
        com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables
          - would restore the DYLD_* injection path that com.twinvpn.twinvpnd's
            pinned PATH and Q10 exist to close.
        com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory
        com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit
          - the MemoryDenyWriteExecute analogue. ADR-0018 §11.16 (f) requires the
            core to link "without writable-executable memory"; a Rust static
            library needs neither.
        com.apple.security.get-task-allow
          - debugger attach. Present in a debug build ONLY; its presence in a
            notarized artifact is a release-blocking defect and
            the `codesign` entitlements dump of SIGNING.md §4 is how it is caught.
        com.apple.security.app-sandbox
          - a system extension is not sandboxed; it runs as root. The App
            Sandbox belongs to the containing app (TwinVPNApp.entitlements).
    -->
</dict>
</plist>
